{
  "audioFile": "audio/temple_voice.mp3",
  "text": "Modern engineers still cannot explain how a single eighty-ton granite block was lifted onto a temple roof one thousand years ago, without machinery. This is the Brihadeeswarar Temple in Thanjavur, India. Built by Emperor Raja Raja Chola the First, in ten-oh-ten A.D. The tower rises two hundred and sixteen feet. And at the very top sits the Kumbam, a single octagonal stone weighing eighty tons. Here is the engineering problem. No crane existed. No iron framework. The nearest granite quarry was sixty kilometers away. Modern structural analysis shows the load path required perfect weight distribution across twelve tiers of diminishing stone courses. The prevailing theory: a six-kilometer-long earthen ramp, angled at just five degrees, used to drag the stone to the summit with elephants and human labor. But here is what baffles engineers. The temple has zero foundation cracks after one thousand years. The Kumbam stone sits without any mortar or binding agent. Only gravity holds it in place. One thousand years. No modern adhesive. Just perfect geometry. They did not have computers. They had mastery.",
